This method treats the staging lane as an open transmission line where a perturbation must physically move from x = 0 to x = L.

The math focuses on manipulating the Group Velocity (vg) of a wave packet through a medium by altering its refractive index relative to angular frequency (omega).

The primary governing equation is the derivative of the optical dispersion relation:

vg = dw/dk = c/ng

Where the Group Index (ng) is defined by the first derivative of the refractive index with respect to frequency:

ng = n(w) + w * (dn/dw)

The Math Execution: By using an infrared pump laser to pre-excite the carbon-black particulates, you artificially force a massive, steep negative gradient (dn/dw < 0) within a narrow spectral window.

The Velocity Consequence: This negative gradient drives the value of ng down to exactly 0.1. Substituting this back into the velocity equation yields:

vg = c / 0.1 = 10 * c

The Distance Function: Because this remains a propagation model, time is a strict linear function of distance (L):Delta_t = L / vg = L / (10 * c)

